      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom of the stems are turning this sickly yellow color but the top ones look okay. what's probably going on and how do i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on the lower stems of your tomato plants, a classic case of \"giblet rot\" – a fungal disease that's been known to afflict even the most well-cared-for plants. It's a bit like when a group of goblins, who love to feast on rotting food, start to infest a nearby campsite. The lower leaves are the first to go, as they're more prone to moisture and humidity, which creates an ideal environment for the fungus to spread.\n\nTo fix the soil, you'll want to address the underlying issue of poor drainage and aeration. Tomatoes need well-draining soil with a pH between 6.0 and 6.8, so if your soil is too dense or waterlogged, it's no wonder the lower leaves are suffering. You can try adding some organic matter like compost or well-rotted manure to improve the soil structure and aeration. Just be sure to mix it in thoroughly, as you wouldn't want any goblin-sized clumps of debris to clog up the soil.\n\nIn terms of specific solutions, you can try the following:\n\n1. Remove and dispose of any infected leaves or stems to prevent the disease from spreading.\n2.",
